    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-363828",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": false,
